// The stop-the-world safepoint — `root.zig`'s `safepointEntry` +
// `safepointImpl` (root.zig:638-750) IN FULL: the mutator handshake (#1629) and
// the COLLECTOR half (#1633), so a Bit-only `libbitrt.a` actually collects.
//
// It was missed by every earlier audit because it is not an `export fn`: root.zig
// publishes it with `comptime { @export(&safepointEntry, .{ .name =
// "bit_rt_safepoint" }); }` (root.zig:753), which a `grep 'export fn bit_rt_'`
// enumeration cannot see. It is also the least optional symbol in the ABI — both
// backends SYNTHESIZE a call to it at every loop back-edge (seed/codegen/x64.zig,
// seed/codegen/arm64.zig), so an image that does not define it does not link.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// WHY IT IS ITS OWN MODULE AND NOT `runtime/gc`'s (#1633 moved it here)
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// #1629 landed it in `runtime/gc` because the mutator handshake is entirely the
// World registry's, which is `../gc/gcworld.bit`'s. The COLLECTOR half cannot
// live there, and the reason is structural rather than stylistic: a collection
// must enumerate root sources owned by `runtime/chan` (buffered `is_ref` channel
// elements, ABI.md §11) and by `runtime/sched` (every other task's stack and
// every task's `arg`, §5), and `runtime/gc` may not import either — `chan`
// imports `../gc`, so the reverse edge is an import cycle (E0044). `extern
// function` is not an escape: it is not on the `@nosplit` allowlist (§10.3) and
// a Linux `--emit-obj` rejects it outright (E0078, the wall SEAM 1 hit at #1600).
//
// So the composition moves to a LEAF module that may import all three, and
// nothing imports it. `../gc/gcworld.bit` keeps sole ownership of the World
// block address and the current-mutator cache — duplicating that cache would let
// one thread hold two slots, which the rendezvous then waits for forever — and
// publishes the two reads this file needs (`worldBlockAddr`, `worldEnsureSelf`).
//
// `runtime/stw` holds ONE file, so the #1511 sort-order rule is trivially
// satisfied; every helper it calls is a pinned symbol in another object.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// THE PIN CYCLE, AND WHY NOTHING HERE CAN CALL ITSELF (#1367)
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// This is the pin-cycle trap (tests/rootpins.zig) in its purest form: the
// backends emit a call to `bit_rt_safepoint` at every loop back edge, so a
// definition of `bit_rt_safepoint` that contains a loop back edge calls ITSELF.
// Pre-rename that call lands harmlessly on the still-linked Zig, so nothing —
// not a build, not a link, not a test run — can see it until G2 (#1583) drops the
// `_root` infix, at which point it is an instant stack overflow in every program
// that loops.
//
// Two independent guarantees, both structural rather than asserted:
//
//   - `safepointEntry` is `@naked` and its body is a single `asm` block. §10.3.1
//     admits no locals, no control flow and no implicit prologue in a naked
//     function, and an `asm` payload is emitted verbatim, so there is no back
//     edge for codegen to attach a poll to. The emitted object carries no
//     relocation to any `bit_rt_` name (checked by `zig build rootpins`, which
//     performs the G2 rename symbolically on the emitted object).
//   - EVERY OTHER function here is `@nosplit`, which suppresses back-edge poll
//     emission outright (§10.3), and whose default-deny allowlist additionally
//     refuses any call to a non-`@nosplit` function (E0075) — so the guarantee is
//     transitive over everything they reach, including the objects this file
//     drives, without anyone having to re-derive the call graph. That matters
//     most for the loops below: this file contains five, and each of them would
//     otherwise be a recursive poll.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// THE ROOT SET, AND WHY IT IS ALL-OR-NOTHING
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// A collection that misses ONE root class does not crash — it sweeps a live
// object and corrupts the heap silently. So the five classes `root.zig`'s
// `scanRoots` (root.zig:406) funnels are enumerated here in the same order, and
// each names where it is implemented:
//
//   1. Buffered `is_ref` channel elements (§11) — `chanScanRegistryRoots`
//      (`../chan/chanreg.bit`, #1602). A buffered word is reachable only through
//      the ring while it sits unreceived. The ring is PROCESS memory, not a
//      GC-traced allocation, so its CONTENTS are the roots.
//   2. THIS thread's own frames, precisely (§4) — `gcScanSnapshot`
//      (`../gc/stackmap.bit`, #1633), walking the stack maps between
//      `stackMapsBegin()`/`stackMapsEnd()`.
//   3. Every OTHER stopped OS thread's frames, precisely — the same walk over
//      each parked mutator's published snapshot (`worldParkedFrame`). The world
//      is stopped, so each of those frames is valid and not moving.
//   4. Every OTHER task's stack, conservatively (§5) — sound because a parked
//      task's callee-saved references are all spilled by the runtime call chain
//      that parked it.
//   5. Every registered task's opaque `arg` (§5/§9) — a spawn thunk is a GC
//      object reachable ONLY through the pending task's `arg` until that task
//      runs and unpacks it.
//
// Classes 4 and 5 are reachable only when a scheduler block is bound; class 1
// only when a channel registry is. `stwBind` takes all three addresses TOGETHER
// and `stwCanCollect` refuses to collect unless every one of them is present —
// deliberately, because "collect with a smaller root set" is the failure mode
// this whole comment exists to prevent. An unbound runtime does not collect; it
// never collects PARTIALLY.

import { worldStopRequested, worldPark, worldRendezvous, worldRestart } from "../gc"
import { worldLockTry, worldLockRelease, worldParkedFrame } from "../gc"
import { worldBlockAddr, worldEnsureSelf, maxMutators } from "../gc"
import { gcShouldCollect, gcMarkBegin, gcCollectFinish } from "../gc"
import { gcStatCollections, gcStatTotalSwept, gcNumObjects } from "../gc"
import { gcMarkConservative, gcScanSnapshot } from "../gc"
import { wordAt, loadWord } from "../gc"
import { chanScanRegistryRoots } from "../chan"
import { taskAt, schedCurrentTask, schedMaxRegistered } from "../sched"
import { schedRegLock, schedRegUnlock, schedRegHead, schedRegNext } from "../sched"
import { ctxSp, taskArg, taskState, taskDone, taskStackBase, taskStackBytes } from "../sched"

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// The bound blocks
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// CALLER-OWNED, the convention every ported runtime block follows (`worldBind`,
// `chanBootBind`): a `@nosplit` module cannot own a block another module must
// also see, so the owner (`runtime/root`, which already holds `gcState`) passes
// the addresses in once at boot. Zero until then, and zero means "do not
// collect" rather than "collect with less".

let gcAddr: i64 = 0
let schedAddr: i64 = 0
let chanRegAddr: i64 = 0

// Collections given up because the stop-the-world rendezvous expired —
// `gc.zig`'s `stats.stw_abandoned`, kept here rather than in the collector block
// because that block is full (`gcWords` = 37, every word assigned) and its size
// is `runtime/root`'s declaration. Abandoning is SAFE (skipping a collection
// only costs heap growth) but is evidence that some thread blocks without the
// `blocked` contract, so it is counted and readable.
let stwAbandoned: i64 = 0

// Statically bounded conservative stack scan (Power of 10): 32MB of words, far
// above the 64KB task stacks the scheduler maps.
const stwMaxStackWords = 4194304

// Bind the three process-wide blocks a collection needs. Called once at boot by
// `runtime/root`, after it has `ptrOf`'d its own collector state, scheduler and
// channel registry (which this `@nosplit` module cannot do for it). The World
// block goes to `worldBind` separately — `../gc/gcworld.bit` owns that one.
export @nosplit @symbol("bit_rt_port_stw_bind") function stwBind(g: int, sched: int, chanReg: int) {
  gcAddr = g
  schedAddr = sched
  chanRegAddr = chanReg
}

// Abandoned-collection count, so a test can assert it stayed 0.
export @nosplit @symbol("bit_rt_port_stw_abandoned") function stwAbandonedCount(): int {
  let n = stwAbandoned
  return n
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// The `BIT_GC_STATS` readings (#1639)
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// `gc.zig`'s `collect` writes `[bit-gc] collection N: ...` to stderr itself.
// THIS PORT CANNOT: every function that can reach a collection is `@nosplit`
// (`runtime/gc` deviation 6 and this file's pin-cycle note), and `@nosplit`
// admits neither `print`, nor an `extern` call, nor a `syscall` (E0075) — so
// nothing on the collector's own path can emit a byte. The line's OWNER is
// therefore the boot code, which is ordinary Bit and has a write primitive, and
// what it needs from here is the numbers.
//
// They are read off the BOUND collector block rather than passed a `g`, because
// the boot module must not be handed the collector state pointer just to print;
// `stwBind` already gave this module the address. Zero before `stwBind`, which
// is the same "nothing to report" answer as zero collections.
//
// The three are pinned rather than re-exported from `runtime/gc`: a
// `--freestanding` module may only reference PINNED symbols (#1628), and
// `runtime/gc`'s observability accessors carry no pin (they are read in-module
// and by same-target stress tests). Pinning them there would widen `runtime/gc`'s
// ABI surface for one caller; forwarding here costs three one-line bodies.

// Collections completed by this collector — `Stats.collections`.
export @nosplit @symbol("bit_rt_port_stw_collections") function stwCollections(): int {
  if (gcAddr == 0) {
    return 0
  }
  return gcStatCollections(wordAt(gcAddr))
}

// Objects swept over the whole run — `Stats.total_swept`. Zero across a run with
// a non-zero collection count means the collector RAN and reclaimed nothing,
// which is the failure a bare count check cannot tell from success.
export @nosplit @symbol("bit_rt_port_stw_swept") function stwSwept(): int {
  if (gcAddr == 0) {
    return 0
  }
  return gcStatTotalSwept(wordAt(gcAddr))
}

// Objects the collector currently holds — the `live=` field.
//
// OBJECT COUNT, NOT `gcLiveBytes`, and the reason is mechanical rather than a
// preference: `gcLiveBytes` reaches the heap through `runtime/gc`'s own private
// `heapBlock`, and the inliner splices its body into this module, so forwarding
// it makes THIS object reference `m2$heapBlock` — a mangled cross-module symbol
// no sibling can define, which `--freestanding` refuses outright (measured).
// The three accessors above and this one are plain word loads, which inline
// cleanly. A count answers the question the stats line is for — did live data
// actually fall across a collection — as directly as a byte total.
export @nosplit @symbol("bit_rt_port_stw_live_objects") function stwLiveObjects(): int {
  if (gcAddr == 0) {
    return 0
  }
  return gcNumObjects(wordAt(gcAddr))
}

// Whether a collection is even permissible: every root source must be reachable.
// See "THE ROOT SET" above — this is the guard that keeps a partially-bound
// runtime from running a collection with a smaller root set than it has roots.
@nosplit function stwCanCollect(): bool {
  if (gcAddr == 0) {
    return false
  }
  if (schedAddr == 0) {
    return false
  }
  if (chanRegAddr == 0) {
    return false
  }
  return true
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Root enumeration
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

// Conservatively mark every aligned word in `[sp, top)` — `root.zig`'s
// `conservativeStack`. Every word goes through `gcMarkConservative`, which
// validates against the heap bounds and an exact ownership test before decoding
// anything, so a small integer that looks like a pointer is simply ignored.
@nosplit function stwScanStackRange(g: *i64, sp: int, top: int) {
  if (sp <= 0) {
    return
  }
  // Align UP, as `std.mem.alignForward` does: a misaligned base would read
  // words straddling two slots, which are not object references in any case.
  let a = (sp + 7) / 8 * 8
  let n = 0
  // Bounded by the mapped stack AND by `stwMaxStackWords`.
  while (a + 8 <= top && n < stwMaxStackWords) {
    gcMarkConservative(g, loadWord(a))
    a = a + 8
    n = n + 1
  }
}

// Classes 4 and 5: every registered task's `arg`, and every task's stack except
// the one this thread is running (scanned precisely from the snapshot instead)
// and any finished task (its stack holds nothing live). `sched.zig`'s
// `forEachTaskArg` + `forEachOtherStack`, fused into one walk because they visit
// the same list under the same lock.
@nosplit function stwScanTasks(g: *i64, sched: *i64) {
  let cur = schedCurrentTask()
  schedRegLock(sched)
  let addr = schedRegHead(sched)
  let n = 0
  // Bounded by `schedMaxRegistered`, `sched.zig`'s own runaway backstop.
  while (addr != 0 && n < schedMaxRegistered) {
    let t = taskAt(addr)
    gcMarkConservative(g, *(t + taskArg))
    if (addr != cur && *(t + taskState) != taskDone) {
      stwScanStackRange(g, *(t + ctxSp), *(t + taskStackBase) + *(t + taskStackBytes))
    }
    addr = schedRegNext(t)
    n = n + 1
  }
  schedRegUnlock(sched)
}

// One full stop-the-world mark-sweep. The world is already stopped and this
// thread holds the world lock; `snap` is this thread's own `SafepointFrame`.
//
// `gcMarkBegin` .. `gcCollectFinish` is `gc.zig`'s `collect` with the root
// callback inverted (`../gc/gccollect.bit` deviation 2) — everything between the
// two is the root enumeration, in `root.zig`'s `scanRoots` order.
@nosplit function stwCollect(g: *i64, world: *i64, selfSlot: int, snap: int) {
  gcMarkBegin(g)

  // 1. Buffered `is_ref` channel elements (§11).
  chanScanRegistryRoots(g, wordAt(chanRegAddr))

  // 2. This thread's own frames, precisely (§4).
  gcScanSnapshot(g, snap)

  // 3. Every other stopped OS thread's frames, precisely.
  let i = 0
  // Bounded: exactly `maxMutators` slots.
  while (i < maxMutators) {
    if (i != selfSlot) {
      let f = worldParkedFrame(world, i)
      if (f != 0) {
        gcScanSnapshot(g, f)
      }
    }
    i = i + 1
  }

  // 4 + 5. Every other task's stack, conservatively, and every task's `arg`.
  stwScanTasks(g, taskAt(schedAddr))

  gcCollectFinish(g)
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// The poll body — `safepointImpl` (root.zig:638), IN FULL
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// `snap` is the address of the caller's `SafepointFrame` (ABI.md §4), which the
// naked shim below reserved on THIS thread's own stack. Per-thread by
// construction — no TLS, no registration, no ordering — which is the #1429 fix;
// the frame used to be one process-wide global and two workers polling at once
// overwrote each other's registers and then both collected.
//
// The frame is valid for exactly as long as this call: it lives below the
// shim's `sp`, so publishing it (`worldPark`) is sound only while this thread
// stays inside the shim, which parking guarantees.
//
// Exactly one of three things happens (ABI.md §5):
//
//  1. Someone else is collecting -> park here until they finish. A mutator that
//     ignored this would run behind the collector's back.
//  2. Nobody is collecting and the trigger is not crossed -> return. The hot
//     path: one atomic load plus the trigger test.
//  3. The trigger is crossed -> try to become the collector.
//
// The `tryAcquire` LOSER PARKS rather than spinning on the lock. That is the
// deadlock-freedom argument, and it is why `worldLockTry` exists alongside the
// spinning `worldLockAcquire`: a thread the collector is waiting for is never
// itself waiting for the collector.
//
// `@nosplit` is REQUIRED, not stylistic — see the pin-cycle note above.
export @nosplit @symbol("bit_rt_port_stw_poll") function stwPoll(snap: int) {
  // Registers this thread if it has not registered yet, and parks it there if a
  // stop is already pending. Publishes the REAL snapshot: `root.zig`'s
  // `currentMutator` passes 0 from the allocation door (runtime code, no stack
  // map) but its real frame from the safepoint door, and parking with 0 would
  // hide exactly the references this poll's stack map names.
  let mu = worldEnsureSelf(snap)

  let waddr = worldBlockAddr()
  if (waddr == 0) {
    return    // pre-boot: `worldBind` has not run, so there is no registry yet
  }
  let world = wordAt(waddr)

  if (worldStopRequested(world)) {
    // Someone is collecting. Park until they finish. `worldPark` returns at
    // once if the stop cleared in the gap, which is what makes this safe to
    // call speculatively. A thread with no slot has nothing to publish and
    // nothing waits for it.
    if (mu != 0) {
      worldPark(world, mu - 1, snap)
    }
    return
  }

  if (!stwCanCollect()) {
    return
  }
  let g = wordAt(gcAddr)
  if (!gcShouldCollect(g)) {
    return
  }

  // A thread with no slot must not collect: `worldRendezvous` skips only the
  // collector's own slot, so a slotless collector would wait for itself forever.
  if (mu == 0) {
    return
  }
  let selfSlot = mu - 1

  if (!worldLockTry(world)) {
    // Another thread is collecting, or is about to. Park speculatively — a
    // no-op if it turns out no stop is pending.
    worldPark(world, selfSlot, snap)
    return
  }

  // Re-test UNDER THE LOCK: the thread that just released it may have collected
  // everything we were about to collect for.
  if (gcShouldCollect(g)) {
    if (worldRendezvous(world, selfSlot)) {
      stwCollect(g, world, selfSlot, snap)
    } else {
      // Could not stop the world within the bound. ABANDON — never force.
      // Skipping a collection only costs heap growth; collecting while a mutator
      // runs costs correctness.
      stwAbandoned = stwAbandoned + 1
    }
    // Restart on BOTH paths: `rendezvous` sets the stop flag before it waits, so
    // an expired rendezvous leaves it set and every mutator that already parked
    // would never resume.
    worldRestart(world)
  }
  worldLockRelease(world)
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// The LIVE entry: only a thread running compiled Bit code is a mutator (#1639)
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// This is what the shim below actually calls, and the only thing it adds to
// `stwPoll` is a gate. The gate is not a tuning knob — it is what keeps the
// registry's ONE current-mutator cache from being shared by two OS threads.
//
// `gcworld.bit`'s `curMut` stands in for `root.zig`'s `threadlocal g_mutator`
// because `@threadlocal` is refused for Mach-O (§11.11). Zig's version is
// per-thread, and no Zig thread outside compiled Bit code ever reaches a
// safepoint, so only ONE thread was ever in it. The Bit port breaks that by
// accident: `boot`'s wait-for-main loop and `joinWorker` are ordinary Bit
// functions, so codegen puts a `bit_rt_safepoint` on their back edges and the
// BOOT thread polls too. Both threads then read and write one `curMut`, and the
// consequence is not a lost collection but a corrupted heap: the boot thread
// would collect using the worker's slot as "its own", and `worldRendezvous` and
// the parked-frame walk both SKIP the collector's own slot — so the worker's
// published frame, the only precise record of the running task's references,
// would never be scanned and every reference in it would be swept.
//
// `schedCurrentTask()` is the discriminator, and it needs no TLS: a green task
// runs on a 64KiB-aligned stack whose base word carries `sched.bit`'s tag, so
// masking the live `sp` answers "am I on a task stack" in two instructions and
// returns 0 on the boot thread and on a worker's own loop stack. That set —
// "executing compiled Bit code on a green thread" — is EXACTLY the set Zig's
// threadlocal admits, so this restores the Zig behaviour rather than narrowing
// it: compiled user code always runs on a task, and runtime code never does.
//
// A gated thread does nothing at all, which is the safe direction: it holds no
// compiled Bit frame, so there is nothing of its to scan, nothing to park for,
// and no reference of its can be missed by a collection it sits out.
//
// SEPARATE FROM `stwPoll` rather than folded into it, deliberately: `stwPoll` is
// the poll body given that the caller IS a mutator, and that is what
// `tests/stress/stwcollect` (#1633) drives directly with a collector block of
// its own — under the still-live Zig scheduler, which writes no stack tag, so a
// gate inside `stwPoll` would silently turn that whole test vacuous.
export @nosplit @symbol("bit_rt_port_stw_safepoint") function stwSafepoint(snap: int) {
  if (schedCurrentTask() == 0) {
    return
  }
  stwPoll(snap)
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// `bit_rt_safepoint` — the naked register/frame snapshot shim (ABI.md §4)
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// Zero-arg, `callconv(.c)`, emitted by both backends at every loop back edge.
// Naked so it runs with NO prologue: it captures the caller's return address,
// frame pointer and callee-saved register file BEFORE any compiled code could
// overwrite them, then hands that snapshot to `stwPoll` as its first C argument.
//
// The `SafepointFrame` layout it builds is root.zig's, field for field:
//   +0   ret        the caller's return address (0 would mean "not captured")
//   +8   fp         the caller's frame pointer
//   +16  regs[32]   regs[n] at +16+8n, physical register n's live value
// `regs` is deliberately NOT zeroed — this runs at every back edge and clearing
// 32 words per poll is not free. Sound because ABI.md §4 restricts the registers
// a stack map may name at a safepoint to exactly the callee-saved file saved
// here, and because every word the walk reads goes through the validating
// `gcMarkRoot`.
//
// The reservation is 8 bytes larger than the 272-byte struct on x86-64 and 16
// larger on AArch64, purely to keep the stack ABI-aligned at the call. A function
// containing a safepoint is non-leaf by construction (it calls this), so it may
// not rely on the x86-64 red zone and overlapping that zone costs nothing.
//
// THE PAYLOAD IS HAND-ENCODED AND DISASSEMBLY-CHECKED, instruction for
// instruction against root.zig's `asm volatile` (root.zig:698-750) — there is no
// text assembler in the toolchain (§11.6), and a wrong offset here does not fail
// a build, it publishes the wrong register as a root.
//
//   x86-64 (SysV; rbx=3, r13=13, r14=14, r15=15 -> +40,+120,+128,+136):
//     48 81 EC 18 01 00 00        subq  $280, %rsp
//     4C 8B 84 24 18 01 00 00     movq  280(%rsp), %r8    # the caller's ret addr
//     4C 89 04 24                 movq  %r8, 0(%rsp)
//     48 89 6C 24 08              movq  %rbp, 8(%rsp)
//     48 89 5C 24 28              movq  %rbx, 40(%rsp)
//     4C 89 6C 24 78              movq  %r13, 120(%rsp)
//     4C 89 B4 24 80 00 00 00     movq  %r14, 128(%rsp)
//     4C 89 BC 24 88 00 00 00     movq  %r15, 136(%rsp)
//     48 89 E7                    movq  %rsp, %rdi        # arg 0 = the frame
//     FF D1                       call  *%rcx
//     48 81 C4 18 01 00 00        addq  $280, %rsp
//     C3                          ret
//   At entry `rsp % 16 == 8` (the caller's `call` pushed the return address), so
//   subtracting 280 restores `rsp % 16 == 0` for the call while leaving the
//   return address at 280(%rsp). `rbp` is still the caller's frame pointer — no
//   prologue ran. `rdi`/`r8`/`rcx` are all caller-saved, so materializing them
//   cannot destroy a callee-saved value before it is saved.
//
//   AArch64 (x19..x28 -> +168..+240):
//     D10483FF   sub  sp, sp, #288
//     F90003FE   str  x30, [sp, #0]        # x30 = the caller's return address
//     F90007FD   str  x29, [sp, #8]        # x29 = the caller's frame pointer
//     A90AD3F3   stp  x19, x20, [sp, #168]
//     A90BDBF5   stp  x21, x22, [sp, #184]
//     A90CE3F7   stp  x23, x24, [sp, #200]
//     A90DEBF9   stp  x25, x26, [sp, #216]
//     A90EF3FB   stp  x27, x28, [sp, #232]
//     910003E0   mov  x0, sp               # arg 0 = the frame
//     F81F0FFE   str  x30, [sp, #-16]!
//     D63F0020   blr  x1
//     F84107FE   ldr  x30, [sp], #16
//     910483FF   add  sp, sp, #288
//     D65F03C0   ret
//   288 keeps `sp` 16-aligned (the hardware enforces it). `x0`/`x1` are the first
//   argument and caller-saved scratch, so neither disturbs the saved file. `x30`
//   is preserved across the call by its own push, NOT by the frame copy, so the
//   return path does not depend on the callee leaving the frame untouched.
//
// `input` materializes `entryOf(stwSafepoint)` into `rcx`/`x1` ahead of the
// payload — the gated live entry, not `stwPoll` itself; see its comment —
// the only compiler-emitted instruction in this function, an inline address
// materialization against a link-time constant (§11.10) that touches no other
// register and no memory. `entryOf` rather than a direct call because §11.6 has
// no relocation for a branch OUT of an `asm` block.
//
// PINNED `bit_rt_root_safepoint`, NOT `bit_rt_safepoint`: root.zig still defines
// the real name and claiming it now is a DuplicateSymbol at every link (#1624).
// G2 (#1583) drops the `_root` infix, as for every other pin in this tree.
@naked @symbol("bit_rt_root_safepoint")
function safepointEntry() {
  return asm volatile {
    x64   { 0x48, 0x81, 0xEC, 0x18, 0x01, 0x00, 0x00,
            0x4C, 0x8B, 0x84, 0x24, 0x18, 0x01, 0x00, 0x00,
            0x4C, 0x89, 0x04, 0x24,
            0x48, 0x89, 0x6C, 0x24, 0x08,
            0x48, 0x89, 0x5C, 0x24, 0x28,
            0x4C, 0x89, 0x6C, 0x24, 0x78,
            0x4C, 0x89, 0xB4, 0x24, 0x80, 0x00, 0x00, 0x00,
